[6.271, "o", "  sufficiency gate: the evidence does NOT support a cause (6.3s); missing: A dated cause (ad platform change log, campaign budget/pause record, billing or account status change) inside the Paid Search account around June 15-16, since the GA4 data only shows Paid Search sessions/conversions collapsed but not why; Google Ads (or other paid channel) campaign change history / budget records for the June window; Confirmation that GA4/analytics tracking itself wasn't altered (e.g. UTM tagging, conversion tracking config) to rule out an instrumentation change rather than a real traffic drop; A non-null, tested significance result for the signup level shift, or a longer post-period so the permutation test has power\r\n"]
[0.007, "o", "\r\n==============================================================================\r\nQ: Why did signups drop in the second half of June?\r\n==============================================================================\r\n\r\nConfidence: insufficient evidence\r\nTook 64s over 5 steps, 27,176 tokens, 14 observations gathered, 11 cited\r\n\r\nANSWER\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Signups dropped from ~56.8/day to ~32.9/day (-42%) starting 2026-06-15 and stayed at that lower level through month end.  [posthog.event_trend 18f4ad93]\r\n  The drop is concentrated in Paid Search: sessions and conversions from that channel fell ~74% on the same date, while Organic Search stayed flat, and no code changes were found in the product or website repos during the window.  [ga4.compare_periods 49bd449f, ga4.get_sessions cd559f23, github.commits 5bb5d08a, github.commits f2f1c90b]\r\n\r\nFINDINGS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Signups fell sharp"]
[0.000, "o", "ly and durably starting June 15  (medium)\r\n    - Daily signups averaged 56.8/day before June 15 and 32.9/day after, a sustained -42.1% level shift beginning 2026-06-15.  [posthog.event_trend 18f4ad93]\r\n  The drop is driven almost entirely by Paid Search, not by an overall funnel or product issue  (medium)\r\n    - Comparing June 16-30 to June 1-15, Paid Search sessions fell 74% (11,070 to 2,876) and Paid Search conversions fell 74% (454 to 118), while Organic Search sessions and conversions were essentially flat (-0.25% and -0.26%).  [ga4.compare_periods 49bd449f]\r\n    - Daily session detail confirms Paid Search sessions dropped abruptly from ~780-790/day (June 1-14) to ~190-196/day starting June 15, the same date the signup level shift begins, while Organic Search stayed at ~620-650/day across the entire month.  [ga4.get_sessions cd559f23, posthog.event_trend 18f4ad93]\r\n\r\nCHARTS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  user signed up over time\r\n    value              |"]
[0.000, "o", "██▇▇███▇▇▇▇▇▇▇▂▂▂▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▂▁▁▁▁|  range 31–60\r\n                        2026-06-01 … 2026-06-30\r\n\r\n  sessions over time\r\n    sessions           |██████████████▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁▁|  range 186–793\r\n                        2026-06-01 … 2026-06-30\r\n\r\n\r\nHYPOTHESES TESTED\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  [contradicted] A product or onboarding defect (e.g. a bad deploy to the signup flow) caused the signup drop.\r\n      No commits were found in acme/product between June 10-30, and Organic Search conversion rate was unchanged, indicating the signup funnel itself was not broken -- only paid traffic volume collapsed.\r\n  [contradicted] A marketing-site change (e.g. to landing pages) broke the paid funnel.\r\n      No commits were found in acme/company-website between June 10-20, ruling out a code change to marketing pages as the driver of the traffic co"]
[0.000, "o", "llapse.\r\n  [inconclusive] Paid Search traffic and its associated conversions collapsed on 2026-06-15 (e.g. a paid campaign was paused or a budget was cut), and this reduction in paid-driven traffic explains the bulk of the signup drop.\r\n      Paid Search sessions and conversions fell ~74% on the exact date (June 15) the signup level shift begins, while Organic Search and (per available GitHub evidence) the product funnel were unaffected. The magnitude of lost Paid Search conversions (-336) is close to the scale of the observed signup decline. No Slack message or annotation records the specific campaign or budget decision, so the proximate marketing action itself is not directly evidenced -- only its footprint in the traffic data.\r\n\r\nRECOMMENDATIONS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  P1 Confirm with the paid marketing/growth team whether a Paid Search campaign or budget was paused/cut around 2026-06-15, and if unintentional, reinstate spend.\r\n      why: Paid Sear"]
[0.000, "o", "ch sessions and conversions fell ~74% exactly when signups fell, and no product or marketing-site code change explains it, making a paid-channel spend change the leading, evidence-backed explanation.\r\n      [ga4.compare_periods 49bd449f, ga4.get_sessions cd559f23]\r\n  P2 Pull the ad platform's own change/billing history (outside the tools available here) to identify the specific campaign, budget, or account action on or around June 15.\r\n      why: No connected source (Slack, GitHub, PostHog annotations) recorded the decision, so the proximate cause is inferred from traffic data alone and should be confirmed at the source.\r\n      [slack.search_messages 6c10df46, slack.find_decision 386b5547]\r\n\r\nRISKS AND CAVEATS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - No Slack message, decision record, or PostHog annotation was found describing a campaign pause or budget cut, despite searching multiple relevant terms. This means the specific action that caused the Paid Search collaps"]
[0.000, "o", "e is inferred from the traffic pattern alone, not confirmed by a change record.\r\n  - Absence of commits in acme/product and acme/company-website for the relevant window is evidence of absence only to the extent GitHub history is complete and the connector saw all repos; it does not itself prove a paid campaign change, since ad platform changes (e.g. Google/Meta Ads) are not visible to any connected tool here.\r\n  - The comparison window (June 1-15 vs June 16-30) is 15 vs 15 days, but the posthog level-shift test flagged that its own before/after split (14 vs 16 days) has no statistical power in that configuration, so the shift is observed but not formally validated against noise.\r\n  - 1 claim(s) were removed because an independent check found the cited evidence did not support them. Treat the remaining conclusions with corresponding caution.\r\n  - 1 claim(s) go beyond their evidence and are retained with reduced confidence rather than removed, because the rest of each one holds. What the check found:\r\n- finding"]
[0.000, "o", "s[0].claims[0]: The numbers (56.8, 32.9, -42.1%, 2026-06-15) match exactly, but the evidence explicitly states 'established' is null and the shift could not be statistically tested, so calling it \r\n  - 0 causal claim(s) were withheld: a separate check of the evidence gathered, made without seeing this report, found that it does not support a definitive answer about cause. The data establishes that Paid Search sessions/conversions and signups both dropped sharply around mid-June, but every search for an explanatory cause (commits, Slack messages about budget/campaign changes, annotations) returned zero rows, and the signup shift itself is explicitly marked as untested/unestablished. This shows correlated timing but no named, dated cause behind it. What would settle it: A dated cause (ad platform change log, campaign budget/pause record, billing or account status change) inside the Paid Search account around June 15-16, since the GA4 data only shows Paid Search sessions/conversions collapsed but not why; Google"]
[0.000, "o", " Ads (or other paid channel) campaign change history / budget records for the June window; Confirmation that GA4/analytics tracking itself wasn't altered (e.g. UTM tagging, conve\r\n\r\nDATA QUALITY\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - The posthog movement-detection test could not formally establish the June 15 level shift as distinguishable from noise given the 14-vs-16-day split; the shift is nonetheless large (-42%) and visible directly in the daily series.\r\n  - 8 of the 11 cited observation(s) returned no results (github.commits, posthog.annotations, slack.find_decision, slack.search_messages). Conclusions resting on them describe what was not found, which is not the same as what does not exist.\r\n\r\nSOURCES\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  49bd449f  ga4.compare_periods  eval://ga4__compare_periods\r\n  cd559f23  ga4.get_sessions  eval://ga4__get_sessions\r\n  5bb5d08a  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  f2f1c90"]
[0.000, "o", "b  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  a47dde3c  posthog.annotations  eval://posthog__annotations\r\n  18f4ad93  posthog.event_trend  eval://posthog__event_trend\r\n  386b5547  slack.find_decision  eval://slack__find_decision\r\n  fa314f58  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n  6c10df46  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n  a3fc61e6  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n  1b2c2fa3  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n"]
